  "seoBody": "<section><span class=\"eyebrow\">Singapore</span><p>Field firms across Singapore already run on WhatsApp. Quickler turns that habit into a compliance system: a field worker answers questions by text, voice note or photo, and every job lands as a clean, structured digital record on a manager dashboard. No app to install, and you only pay for active users, no paper to chase back to the office.</p></section><h2>A structured record, not a photo dump</h2><p>The hard part of field compliance in Singapore is rarely doing the work. It is proving it afterwards: collating photos from WhatsApp groups, retyping handwritten checklists, and rebuilding a defensible record when a building owner, a main contractor or an auditor asks for one.</p><p>Quickler removes the retyping. Each answer a worker sends becomes a field in a live, machine-readable record. The manager dashboard shows fault status at a glance:</p><ul><li><strong>Red</strong> for failures that need action now.</li><li><strong>Amber</strong> for items to watch or schedule.</li><li><strong>Green</strong> for everything that passed.</li></ul><p>When a formal document is needed, a clean PDF exports straight from that record. The record is the product; the PDF is a convenience, not the other way round.</p><h2>The tool your crews already open</h2><p>Singapore's field-services landscape is dense and multi-trade: high-rise facilities management, lift and escalator maintenance, fire protection, M&amp;E contracting, building services across HDB estates and commercial towers, plus fleet and lone-worker checks for the firms that service them.</p><p>These crews already coordinate over WhatsApp. Asking them to learn a separate inspection app adds friction and training cost, and the app is the first thing that gets skipped under time pressure.</p><p>Quickler meets them where they are. The worker has a normal WhatsApp conversation; the structure happens behind the scenes. Adoption is close to zero-effort because there is nothing new to open.</p><h2>One platform across Singapore's field trades</h2><h3>Inspections and statutory checks</h3><p>Buildings &amp; facilities. Fire safety walks, lift and escalator condition checks, electrical and M&amp;E inspections, and routine facilities rounds. Upload your existing form and quickler builds it into a WhatsApp workflow, rooted in real UK compliance forms such as the EICR and built to carry whatever your scope demands.</p><h3>Van, plant and lone-worker checks</h3><p>Mobile &amp; fleet. Daily vehicle checks, plant and equipment inspections, and lone-worker confirmations from the road or the site, captured by voice note and photo without a worker breaking stride.</p><h3>Sign-off you can hand over</h3><p>Certificates. Where a job ends in a certificate, the structured record carries the on-site acknowledgement and exports as a clean PDF. See the full range on the <a href=\"/pages/services.html\">Workflows</a> page.</p><h2>Quickler in Singapore, answered</h2><h3>Is there an app to install?</h3><p>No. Quickler runs over WhatsApp, the tool your crews already use every day. Workers answer by text, voice note or photo. There is no download and only pay for active users.</p><h3>What do I get at the end?</h3><p>A clean, structured digital record on a manager dashboard, with red, amber and green fault status. A PDF exports from it whenever you need a formal document.</p><h3>Will it fit our own forms?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler is job-agnostic. Upload any inspection or checklist form and it becomes a WhatsApp workflow, across electrical, fire, facilities, lift, HVAC, plumbing and fleet work.</p><h3>Where does the data live?</h3><p>On EU servers in Germany, GDPR-aligned. Built to work anywhere WhatsApp does and used across markets including the UK and beyond. Read more on the <a href=\"/pages/global-field-compliance.html\">global field compliance</a> page.</p>",
